              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "stefanskiasan"
            },
            "body": "We can confirm this fix from production use. We have been running DSpark speculative decoding with Kimi-K3 on an 8x MI350X node (gfx950, TP8, `--speculative-dspark-block-size 4`, `--speculative-num-draft-tokens 5`) for several days, with the same `is_hip()` guard applied locally in `dflash_utils.py`. With the guard in place, accept length is ~2-5 depending on content/context length, and single-stream decode throughput improves accordingly — matching the CUDA behavior this PR describes. Without it, the silent greedy fallback is indeed invisible in logs, which made this painful to diagnose independently.\n\nTwo operational data points from production on ROCm that may be useful for reviewers:\n\n- DSpark composes fine with TP8 + fp8 KV cache (`kv_cache_dtype=fp8_e4m3`) and full CUDA-graph decode on gfx950.\n- Combining DSpark with MoE EP hits an unrelated per-rank mamba state pool sizing limit in the current tree, so DSpark+EP should not be considered covered by this PR's testing — TP-only is the well-trodden path.\n\nThanks for upstreaming this — it removes a nasty silent-fallback foot-gun.",
